  "result": "No, that's not right. A language model doesn't remember earlier conversations on its own, so your assistant won't automatically remember what a student said last week.\n\nPicture a stateless API endpoint. Each request gets only the data in its payload, and the model works the same way. Suppose Maya says on Monday, \"I finished the laser cutter training.\" On Friday she starts a new session. The model gets the new prompt and nothing else, so it has no idea she ever said that.\n\nThis is tempting because inside one chat it does look like memory. But your code resends the earlier messages with every call. Across sessions, nothing is resent unless you build it.\n\n**Why this works:** the model only reads the text in its current context. \"Memory\" means your code saves information, for example a training-status record in a database, and puts the relevant part back into the prompt next time.\n\nThat's your design decision from the mission: what to save between sessions. In your own words, what would the assistant need to save about Maya, and where would that data sit when she returns on Friday?",
